<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Write seven whole numbers, smallest first, so that the median is <m>{30}</m>, the lower quartile is <m>{22}</m>, the upper quartile is <m>{32}</m> and the mean is <m>{30}</m>.</p><p>Type the seven numbers in square brackets, separated by commas, for example <c>[3,5,8,9,12,14,20]</c>.</p><div><fillin characters="25" name="ans1"/> </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>For seven values in order, <m>n + 1 = 8</m>, so the median is at position <m>8/2 = 4</m>, <m>Q_1</m> at <m>8/4 = 2</m> and <m>Q_3</m> at <m>3 \times 8/4 = 6</m>. These positions are whole numbers, so fix the 2nd, 4th and 6th numbers as <m>{22}</m>, <m>{30}</m> and <m>{32}</m>.</p><p>Choose the 1st, 3rd and 5th numbers to keep the list in order, for example <m>{21}</m>, <m>{29}</m> and <m>{31}</m>. Seven numbers with mean <m>{30}</m> add to <m>7 \times {30} = {210}</m>, so the 7th number is what is left: <m>{210} - {165} = {45}</m>, which is at least <m>{32}</m>, as it must be.</p><p>One answer is <m>{\left[ 21 , 22 , 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 , 45 \right]}</m>. Any list of whole numbers in order with these 2nd, 4th and 6th numbers and total <m>{210}</m> is correct.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left[ 21 , 22 , 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 , 45 \right] </m></p></answer>
</stack-static>